From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hermes <hermes@local>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 11:31:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] host: the address the host resolves is in host order, like
 every other
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The redirect table hands the engine an address as a number, and that number
is host order everywhere: the map's own rules read 10.0.0.9 as 0x0A000009 and
the guest sockaddr writer puts the octets out from it with shifts. The
resolver copied sin_addr in instead, which is network order, so on a
little-endian device the four bytes came back reversed and the title dialled
the mirror image of the server: 194.29.178.51 for nex.nextendo.network, whose
real address is 51.178.29.194. Nothing answers there, the non-blocking connect
runs out its whole budget and the online flow stops with 0x270141 — which is
what the phone's log showed, address and all.

The TLS layer's read of the peer address from a guest sockaddr gets the same
conversion, or it would look the SNI name up under the reversed number.

Upstream-Status: fork-only
Reason: The redirect table hands the engine an address as a host-order number: the map's own rules read 10.0.0.9 as
 0x0A000009 and the guest sockaddr writer shifts the octets out of it. The resolver copied sin_addr in instead, which is
 network order, so on a little-endian device the four bytes came out reversed and the title dialled the mirror image of
 the server (194.29.178.51 for nex.nextendo.network, whose address is 51.178.29.194). Nothing answers there, so the
 non-blocking connect spends its whole budget and the online flow stops with 0x270141. The TLS layer's read of the
 peer address from a guest sockaddr gets the same conversion, or the SNI name is looked up under the reversed number.
Issue: https://github.com/GigiFrack/NX2Apple (fork)

---
 dm/host/hle_nextendo.c         | 6 +++++-
 dm/host/hle_ssl.c              | 6 +++++-
 dm/host/tests/t_nextendo_map.c | 6 ++++++
 3 files changed, 16 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/dm/host/hle_nextendo.c b/dm/host/hle_nextendo.c
index 8718a4d7..b4423264 100644
--- a/dm/host/hle_nextendo.c
+++ b/dm/host/hle_nextendo.c
@@ -91,6 +91,10 @@ void dm_nd_reload(void) {
 static uint32_t g_nd_nex_ip, g_nd_nat_ip;
 static int g_nd_builtin_done;
 
+// The number every other part of the engine passes around is the address in HOST order (10.0.0.9 is
+// 0x0A000009, as the map's own parser and the guest sockaddr writer both assume). `sin_addr` sits in
+// memory in network order, so copying it as an integer hands out the same four bytes backwards: on a
+// little-endian device 51.178.29.194 became 194.29.178.51 and the TCP connect went to a stranger.
 static uint32_t nd_resolve_name(const char *name) {
     struct addrinfo hints, *res = NULL;
     memset(&hints, 0, sizeof hints);
@@ -101,7 +105,7 @@ static uint32_t nd_resolve_name(const char *name) {
     for (struct addrinfo *a = res; a; a = a->ai_next) {
         if (a->ai_addr && a->ai_addrlen >= sizeof(struct sockaddr_in)) {
             const struct sockaddr_in *sin = (const struct sockaddr_in *)a->ai_addr;
-            memcpy(&ip, &sin->sin_addr.s_addr, sizeof ip);   // already network order
+            ip = ntohl(sin->sin_addr.s_addr);
             break;
         }
     }
diff --git a/dm/host/hle_ssl.c b/dm/host/hle_ssl.c
index e954b68d..1606a9ff 100644
--- a/dm/host/hle_ssl.c
+++ b/dm/host/hle_ssl.c
@@ -226,7 +226,11 @@ static int dm_ssl_open(dm_ssl_conn *c) {
         uint32_t peer = 0;
         unsigned n = 0;
         unsigned char sa[16];
-        if (c->fd >= 0 && dm_nd_tcp_name(c->fd, sa, &n, 1) == 0 && n >= 8) memcpy(&peer, sa + 4, 4);
+        if (c->fd >= 0 && dm_nd_tcp_name(c->fd, sa, &n, 1) == 0 && n >= 8) {
+            // The guest sockaddr carries the octets in order; the map answers in host order.
+            memcpy(&peer, sa + 4, 4);
+            peer = ntohl(peer);
+        }
         sni = dm_nd_host_for_addr(peer);
         if (!sni) sni = dm_nd_last_host();
     }
diff --git a/dm/host/tests/t_nextendo_map.c b/dm/host/tests/t_nextendo_map.c
index 4c52ee1a..6ec9419f 100644
--- a/dm/host/tests/t_nextendo_map.c
+++ b/dm/host/tests/t_nextendo_map.c
@@ -61,6 +61,12 @@ int main(void) {
     expect(strcmp(dm_nd_sni_for("gs.nintendo.net"), "gs.nintendo.net") == 0, "anything else is left alone");
     expect(dm_nd_lookup("*.example.org", got, 4) == 0, "a bad address never becomes a rule");
 
+    // An address that comes from the host's resolver enters the same convention as the map's own rules:
+    // host order, the way 10.0.0.9 above is 0x0A000009. Copied straight out of sin_addr it arrived with
+    // its four bytes backwards, and the title dialled 194.29.178.51 instead of 51.178.29.194 — a connect
+    // that runs out its whole budget against a machine that has nothing to say.
+    expect(nd_resolve_name("51.178.29.194") == 0x33B21DC2u, "the resolver answers in host order, as the map does");
+
     setenv("DM_NEXTENDO", "0", 1);
     dm_nd_reload();
     expect(dm_nd_rule_count() == 0, "no rules with the switch off");
